hi there steve kaufman here  you know today i'm going to talk about  sort of  the pace  the amount of time how much you know  with what intensity we study languages  and and things that influence that and  i'm going to start by confessing that i  haven't been doing much  arabic learning the last little while  so first of all the reason for that is  that  first of all i had a new floor laid here  in our  little house so that disrupted my  routine  second of all  my wife discovered this  spanish  serial on netflix  called gran hotel and we've been  watching that for two three hours every  evening  so that takes away from my arabic study  time  and it's just a series of things and  then next week we have people coming to  stay with us uh swedish friends  people that i um you know worked with uh  in the lumber business for over 20 years  at least the husband there and they're a  couple coming to visit with us and  that's certainly going to limit the  amount of time i have for arabic  and so it got me to thinking about this  whole issue that when we study languages  when i study a language especially today  i'm studying it for myself  and  there will be periods when for one  reason or another you can't get at you  can't find the time you can't fit it in  and  you know it's unpleasant to put pressure  uh on yourself  and uh you know if i think back for  example  unfortunately now that i've started  learning more languages and i'm putting  up videos every two  or twice a week  i i feel now a greater sense of  obligation to progress in a language  but  when i started all this stuff with  russian which was the first sort of  language that i decided to go after  i mean i think i can't remember but i  think i went a year or more before i  even spoke the language and and i had no  pressure on no one was watching me i  just learn it when i feel like it i i  listen to what i want to read what i  want and it was tremendously enjoyable  when i went from russia to check  even there 